ece of dna  but then it will fall off  and what this sequencing technique  allows for is  multiple tens of thousands of reads of  circular pieces of dna  and this technique shown in panel b can  sequence up to 30 kilobases of dna and  extremely long length of dna  in a single reed and this is very  exciting technology because as shown in  the previous slide every single one of  our double mutant combinations  was very far apart in the gene and so we  thought this could be a technique that  could actually help us  figure out if these mutations are in the  same allele or not  now working with our friends  at mount sinai school of medicine and  bobby sebra  we devised a protocol where we initially  identified metastatic patients  who had double pixel mutations using a  next generation sequencing platform  msk impact we consented them to our  biospecimen protocol  and these patients graciously underwent  fresh metastatic tissue biopsy  and we performed the workflow shown here  we first  optimized this workflow to determine the  allele configuration of  several parental cancer cell lines  that have multiple pic3ca mutations and  in data that i'm not showing you here  we determined that many of these cell  lines have  mutations that insist on the same allele  and some of them actually have mutations  in trans  but we were able to use this workflow to  optimize this technique after optimizing  this technique  we turned to our patient samples  to determine the allele configuration of  the double mutations  and this is actually our data  of each of six patients bearing  six different double pca mutation  combinations and the bottom line is that  all of these mutations are in cysts on  the same allele  i'd like to walk you through a little  bit of this data in the first row  we have a patient with e545k e726k  this is the most frequent double  mutation combination that we see in  breast cancer  and looking at the colored bars on the  right you can see that 56.1 excuse me  58.1 percent of amplicons  contain mutations in cis on the same  allele which are shown in the red  squares  now 39.5 of the amplicons  have wild-type dna this is to be  expected because  breast or all tumor biopsies are not 100  pure so we're going to have some wild  type dna in there but only two percent  contain  amplicon contained e726k alone shown in  yellow  and only 0.2 in this sample contained e5  or 5k mutations in trans and if we march  down to these other patient samples you  can see that the vast majority of the  mutant amplicons are insists on the same  allele  we also used orthogonal techniques such  as colony sequencing  next generation sequencing and rna  sequencing to validate  rare double mutations that are actually  very close together in the gene  using these short read techniques  and to our knowledge this is the first  use of smrt sequencing  to phase multiple alterations in solid  tumors  and therefore all of these data together  show that at least in breast cancer the  vast majority of double pca mutations  are in cysts on the same allele  now armed with this data that these  mutations were insist  we proceeded to functionally  characterize the effects of double  mutation  we focused in panel a on the mutation  combination sewn in the second gray box  and in all of these functional assays we  tested  the wild type gene mutation a  mutation b and mutation a plus b  and so in panel b we assembled a series  of isogenic  cell lines these are cell lines uh that  only differ  in their pic we see a mutation and  otherwise are genetically similar  and we performed western blotting  looking at cell signaling pathways and  as you can see in the western blot  double mutations for as i'm showing here  double mutations have increased  signaling in the pi3 kinase pathway  both proximally at the level of fosso  akt  and distally at the level of phosphos6  what's pretty striking is that at the  level of fosso s6 you can see that  the western blotting that these bands  are  uh only present in the double mutants  and are really not present in the wild  type or the single mutant  and so therefore double mutations  increase pi3 kinase pathway signaling  compared to single mutations  we also have similar findings in  different cell lines here we tested  mcf10a cells these are breast  normal epithelial breast cells but we  also have similar findings using nih3t3  cells which are mouse fibroblasts  as well as mcf7 er positive breast  cancer cells  we looked at signaling kinetics that is  signaling over different time points and  we found that at a 48 hour time point  the double mutants have increased  signaling compared to the single mutants  and wild type as shown at the  in this series of western blots  we tested we next tested to see if this  increase in signaling correlates with an  increase in cell growth and we  we see that yes that this is true you  can see that the double mutants have  increased cell growth in panel a by  crystal violet staining  and this is quantified in panel b and  here we're starting to see  synergistic effects where the effects of  the double mutant are much greater than  the additive effects of each constituent  single mutant alone  we also tested if these mutations  expressed in  trans that is on separate alleles what  the effects of these are and we found  that  in trans these double mutations  have similar signaling levels to the  single mutant as shown in the left-hand  panel and do not have  increased cell signaling or using  increased cell growth in the right-hand  panel  compared to the mutations in cis  we looked at the effects of our  mutations in  mice in mouse models by creating  allograft  allograft that is taking our cell lines  and injecting them into mice and  monitoring for tumor growth  and as you can see the double mutant  shown in light blue e726k  h1047r this has increased this confers  increased tumor growth compared to  single mutants  and wild type in a mouse model and also  leads to increased cell signaling as  shown in western blotting in panel b  and by immunohistochemistry in panel c  next we wanted to investigate the  mechanism of how  the pi3 kinase complex is modified by  these double mutations  and so to do this i'd like to first take  a long a big step back  to investigate or to to discuss what's  known about how these two different  proteins  p110 and p85 function and work from  jonathan backers group  very elegant work has shown that p110  which is a catalytic subunit again coded  by pic3ca  is both stabilized and inhibited by p85  the regulatory subunit  and this is shown in panel a this is  pulse chase showing that  p110 by itself degrades  at about two hours while in the presence  of p85  this complex is stable for at least five  hours  and this increase in stability  correlates with a  decrease in activity as shown in panel b  and this is quantified in panel c where  p110 by itself has high catalytic  activity  in the dotted line when bound to p85  this catalytic activity is decreased  and is partially rescued with the  addition of a phosphopeptide  now the addition of a phosphopeptide is  a  mimic of what happens normally in a cell  where a receptor tyrosine kinase such as  the insulin receptor  is phosphorylated and then binds to pi  pi3 kinase  for downstream signaling and this  phosphopeptide binding also leads to  increased lipid binding  now this graph this is just a pictorial  representation of the data that i showed  you in the prior panel  uh that in the presence of p85 the wild  type pi3 kinase complex has low levels  of kinase activity  and that sequentially with the addition  of fossil peptide this opens up the  protein kinase complex  leading to increased lipid binding and  when p110  is by itself that is as a monomer  it has very high catalytic activity so  now amidst this framework  how do the single hotspot mutations  e545k and h1047r  function and this is a pictorial  representation of a number of  seminal beautiful biochemical and  biophysical experiments  done by many groups over the years and  what these groups have shown  uh notably mario ansel's group and roger  williams's group  is that e545k functions as what we term  a disruptor  it disrupts the inhibitory interactions  between p85 and p110  opening up the protein complex and  other mutations that are at the p110 p85  binding  interface have a similar mechanism of  action  in contrast h-1047-r which is located in  the c-terminus at the  at the end of the p-110 protein  functions as what we call a binder it  increases lipid membrane binding  and this is pictorially represented here  and other mutations that are in the  c-terminus of p-110  have a similar mechanism of action so  therefore  this has established the most frequent  hotspot mutation mechanism  and now what is the mechanism of these  other second  spot mutations that we identified  in panel a this is a domain schematic  showing the location  in green of the second site mutations  453  726 and 1043. in pedal b  this is the crystal structure with all  of these five uh three different  mutational positions  and five four five ten four seven shown  as black spheres  as you can see four five three e5453 is  located at the binding interface between  p110  in blue and p85 and orange it's right  between the orange and the blue  same thing with five sides uh  1043 is located very close to 1047  in the c-terminus of pic3ca  and 726 is located in this loop here  far away from these other mutation sites  therefore we speculated  that e453q functions as what we  determine disruptor just like e545k  and m1043l functions what we term  as what we term binder like h1047r  however e726k nothing was known about  what this mutation might be doing  but based on the crystal structure shown  in panel b as well as in this slide  showing the alignment of multiple  crystal structures  you can see that e726 is located in this  loop that is  right by the putative lipid membrane  plane  now e726 is an uh amino acid called  glutamic acid which is acidic  and is negatively charged if mutated to  a k which is a lysine residue lysine is  a positively charged amino acid  now this is important because lipid  membrane phospholipids are negatively  charged phospholipids  and so we would predict that if we have  a mutation to a positively charged amino  acid  this may increase affinity with a  negatively charged phospholipid  in a membrane therefore we speculated  that e726 would function as a binder  and together these classical papers in  the field combined with our  investigation of the crystal structure  vis-a-vis the mutations that we have  identified in our study led us to the  hypothesis that double mutants activate  pi3-kinase by both  increasing p85 disinhibition that is  increasing the disruption  as well as increasing membrane binding  now to investigate this hypothesis  we prepared recombinant full-length  human pi3 kinase mutant protein  complexes them  complexes and subjected them to a  variety of biochemical experiments  to investigate p85 disinhibition we  performed an assay called thermal shift  this is an assay that measures protein  destabilization  and we interpret that as a proxy for p85  disinhibition  this is the western blotting what we do  is we take our proteins and heat them on  a temperature gradient from 46 degrees  to 63 degrees  and the protein complexes are blotted by  western blotting  to look for the temperature at which the  protein denatures  you can see that wild type we see five  spots  at our on our temperature gradient but  only four spots with e545k  showing that e545k denatures at a lower  temperature than wild type and this is  the raw data  of our experiments and as you can see  the double mutants which is shown in the  lower  the lowest row in each panel the double  mutants  denature at a lower temperature than the  single mutants or wild type  this is quantified here in panel a where  you can see that the double mutants have  lower melting temperatures than the  single mutants or wild type  in panel b we performed lipid kinase  acids using radioactivity  directly measuring the product of the  kinase reaction and we can  we we showed that the double mutants  have increased lipid kinase activity  compared to the single mutant  and finally we looked at lipid binding  by preparing  liposomes that is circular vesicles of  lipids  with different lipid species and we  measured their binding with our  recombinant pi3 kinase protein complexes  and as you can see in penal c in the  western blocks which is quantified in  panel d  all the double mutants had increased  lipid binding compared to the single  mutants or wild type  therefore putting all of these data  together these  biochemical data suggests that double  pcc mutations increase kinase activity  through a disruptor binder combination  mechanism  next we wanted to investigate in our  cellular models  how double picture mutations may modify  sensitivity  to pi3 kinase inhibitors  we took our cell lines and exposed them  to our pellets  the western blocks i'm showing you here  are short-term one-hour drug treatments  and as you can see in the absence of  drug as shown in the minus  columns the double mutants have  increased picc kinase signaling compared  to single mutants  however in the presence of drug as shown  in the plus columns  the signaling is abrogated under all  conditions  and we showed similar findings in our  other cellular models  now this was a short-term drug treatment  for one hour however when we expose  these cells to much  longer durations of drug treatment in  this  experiment four days you can see that  the double mutants  are actually more sensitive to pi3  kinase inhibitors than single mutants  for clarity i've broken this up into the  double mutants containing e545k  in the top panel and the double mutants  containing h1047r  in the bottom panel in the top panel you  can see that  the double e 5 4 5 k e 7 26 k and e 5 4  5 k  m 10 43 l as shown in the purple and  brown  curves are more sensitive that is uh  their cells are killed with a lower  concentration of drug compared to e545k  and in turn e545k is more sensitive than  these rare single mutants found rarely  by themselves in rescue  and this is true at the bottom with the  h1047r containing double mutant  and these patterns of drug sensitivity  by western blotting  and by cell viability assays were  recapitulated  uh showing almost identical results  using a structurally dissimilar  pi3 kinase inhibitor gdc007 which is now  in late stage clinical trials  we also tested our transmutant cells and  showed that the transmutants are not in  fact more sensitive to alpella sub  versus single mutants  in this curve in these curves the  transmutant is shown in the light blue  dotted line and you can see that these  the drug sensitivity of the transmutant  is very similar to the single mutant  finally we wanted to validate our  hypothesis  connecting and correlating the increased  response  of double pictures in tumors to pisce  kinase inhibitors  in a clinical setting  and to do this we teamed up with  genentech  to perform a secondary analysis of the  sandpiper clinical trial  now this clinical trial was similar to  the solar one trial  regarding patient inclusion criteria it  had a very similar patient population  and this trial tested the addition of  tiscellosim  a pi3 kinase alpha inhibitor to  fulvestrine which is an er degrader  in pic3ca mutant metastatic estrogen  receptor positive breast cancer  this trial enrolled 631 patients and  339 patients had pic3ca mutations  of those 339 patients shown in gray 66  patients shown in blue  had multiple picture mutations while 273  patients shown in orange  had one pcc mutation i'd like to point  out that  for this analysis we used circulating  tumor dna  to determine pc3 mutational status and  we did this because  many patients with er positive breast  cancer  have bone only metastases and bone  biopsies are very difficult to analyze  due to the need for decalcification  and so therefore we use ct dna which we  rationalized would allow us to analyze a  larger number of patients  this is a so-called waterfall plot of  the patients on the tesla sub arm of the  sandpiper trial  each var represents a single patient and  positive y-axis shown in the left-hand  side  correlates with two corresponds with  tumor growth whereas  negative on negative y-axis corresponds  with  a tumor shrinkage now first ignoring the  colors we can see  that more patients had tumor shrinkage  than tumor growth this is consistent  with the fact that this was a positive  clinical trial again this drug did not  move forward in the drug discovery  process due to increased toxicity  and then looking at the colors we can  see that multiple mutant patients shown  in blue  have more of a increased tumor shrinkage  compared to single mutant patients shown  in orange  and this is quantified in this in these  bar graphs in panel a this is the total  mutant population or the total  uh population of patients with pixie  mutations  you can see an increased response rate  in with the addition of testosin  and this is the whole patient population  and when we break it up into patients  with  single pictures of mutant tumors versus  multiple pigments immune tumors  we see that the patients with single  picture tumors had an  increased response rate but this was not  statistically significant  while the patients with multiple picture  mutant tumors shown in blue  had an increased response rate which was  statistically significant  now i'd like to point out that of course  this was not the primary endpoint of  this  sandpiper clinical trial to investigate  double  the responsive double pca mutant  patients  but this reflects the best available  clinical data at this time to  ascertain and and elucidate our  hypothesis  and we await publication of the  sandpiper clinical trial  to determine the effective double pcn  mutations on  progression-free survival in this  population  and so together these data suggest what  we term a  mutational dosage model for pi3 kinase  activation and inhibition in breast  cancer  wild type pi3 kinase alpha as shown in  the third row  of this box leads to increased akt  signaling while mutant pi3 kinase leads  to even more increased akt signaling and  double  double mutant pi free kinase leads to  even more akt signaling and increased  cell growth and this is important  because it correlates  with a clinically meaningful phenotype  of increased response to pi3 kinase  alpha inhibitors  for double p acme chinese mutant tumors  and these data points to a  second mechanism for oncogene activation  of double pca mutations in cysts  and so in conclusion i've shown you that  double pca mutations are a novel and  frequent genomic alteration in pc and  mutant cancers  they're insist on the same allele  they activate pi3 kinase signaling and  growth more than single mutants  we've shown this in a variety of models  and they do this through a novel  combination  disruptor binder biochemical mechanism  we showed that cysteine increased  response to pi3 kinase alpha  inhibitors compared to single mutants in  breast cancer  and we believe that this is a rationale  to test pi3 kinase alpha inhibitors  in multiple pcc immune cancers  with this i would i would like to  acknowledge many people  jose baselga and mauricio scout for  providing an exceptional laboratory  environment to investigate this question  lou cantley's lab at wild cornell for  tremendous help with our biochemistry  experiments  my fellow breast oncologist on the  memorial sloan-kettering  cancer center breast medicine service  for helping me obtain patient samples  my many other collaborators at mskcc  bobby sebred melissa smith at mount  sinai genome sciences  they were uh tremendously helpful  and and critical for helping us with the  single molecule real time sequencing or  smrt seek  my other collaborators well rabadon more  and more dickler  lori friedman previously at genentech  and the entire  group at genentech for collaborating  with us on the sandpiper clinical trial  analysis and finally to the many  patients all over the world who have  obtained biopsies  with us and have enrolled on pip kinase  inhibitor trials over the years  this is a list of my funding sources and  at this time  i would be happy to take any questions  thank you very much


